1. Scope and ownership

"Engine" means the original OpenDLE: Studio (OpenDLE) code and documentation made
available by the Licensor with this license. Third-party software, reference
trees, game content, names and trademarks are excluded and retain their own
terms. This license cannot grant rights the Licensor does not own or control.
The Licensor retains ownership of the Engine. You retain ownership of your
original games, assets and modifications, subject to rights in the Engine.
This is a custom source-available license, not an open-source license.

2. Permissions

Subject to these terms, you may inspect the Engine source, privately use and
modify the Engine, develop games, and distribute and sell those games with
the runtime components necessary to run them. Browser-delivered runtime code
is permitted as part of a game; this is not permission to distribute an engine
editor, engine SDK, independent engine product or general-purpose engine fork.
You are not required to publish the source code of your games or your private
modifications to the Engine.
Preserve Engine copyright/license notices and supply this license with any
Engine components distributed with a game.

3. Source redistribution and forks

Except for game runtime distribution under section 2 and contributions under
section 6, you may not publish, distribute, sublicense or sell the Engine or
its modified source, independent engine forks, editors or SDKs without separate
written permission. Private modifications are allowed.

If the Licensor publishes a public repository on a hosting platform, viewing,
copying or forking strictly as permitted by that platform's applicable terms
is not prohibited by this license. Such platform permissions do not grant
additional rights to publish independent Engine releases or avoid royalties.
Contribution-only forks necessary to submit changes to the official repository
are permitted for that purpose, not as independent maintained distributions.

4. Per-game royalties

All amounts and thresholds below are in United States dollars (USD).
"Game" includes the same game's releases on different platforms, editions,
ports, DLC and related sales. Account for that Game across your controlled
publishing entities; a new edition, publisher or store account does not restart
its threshold. Do not include unrelated games or unrelated business income.

"Receipts" means amounts received by you or on your behalf from exploitation
of the Game, after deducting store/payment processing fees, sales taxes
collected for government authorities, refunds and chargebacks. Include game
sales, DLC, in-game purchases, advertising, sponsorships, donations attributable
to the Game and game licensing receipts.
Do not deduct development costs, wages, marketing costs, income taxes or these
royalties. This basis is receipts, not accounting profit. Do not deduct any
amount twice. Convert non-USD receipts consistently using a documented exchange
rate applicable when received.

Zero-receipts games are permanently royalty-exempt under this version of the
license: a Game that generates no Receipts throughout its lifetime owes the
Licensor no royalty, catch-up payment, minimum fee, license fee or royalty
reporting obligation. No fee or tax payable to the Licensor is imposed merely
for creating, distributing or playing such a Game. This does not exempt anyone
from taxes or obligations owed to government authorities or other parties.

"Free", "non-profit" or charitable status alone does not determine eligibility.
A free-to-download Game with advertising, donations, sponsorships or optional
purchases generating Receipts follows the normal threshold rules. If a
previously zero-receipts Game later generates Receipts, apply those rules from
its first Receipts; no royalty is charged for the earlier zero-receipts period.
A later zero-receipts month does not cancel already accrued royalties or the
reporting obligations of a Game that previously reached the threshold.

Before cumulative lifetime Receipts for a Game reach USD 10,000, no royalty
is payable. When they first reach USD 10,000, a USD 200 catch-up royalty becomes
payable. Every dollar of Receipts above USD 10,000 is also subject to a 2%
royalty.

Calculate the USD 10,000 threshold separately for each Game by adding that
Game's qualifying Receipts over its entire lifetime, across all months and
years. The total does not reset when a new month or year begins. For example,
USD 6,000 in one year plus USD 4,000 in the next year reaches the threshold
and triggers the USD 200 catch-up royalty.

After the threshold is reached, there is no new USD 10,000 allowance in later
months or years. Subsequent Receipts remain subject to the 2% royalty even in
a month with only USD 100 in Receipts (USD 2 royalty), subject to the refund
and credit rules below. The USD 200 catch-up royalty is triggered only once
for that Game. A separate Game has its own lifetime threshold, but editions,
platform releases, ports and DLC of the same Game share that Game's threshold
as defined above.

Examples (no subsequent refunds):
  Lifetime Receipts USD 9,999: total royalty USD 0.
  Lifetime Receipts USD 10,000: total royalty USD 200.
  Lifetime Receipts USD 12,000: total royalty USD 240.
  A subsequent month's Receipts of USD 3,000: additional royalty USD 60.

Pay the USD 200 catch-up amount within 30 days after the end of the calendar
month in which the threshold is reached, even if no further sales occur.
It is not deferred until additional money is earned. In that month also pay
2% of the portion above the threshold. In later months report and pay 2% of
that month's Receipts within 30 days after month end. Do not charge 2% again
on the first USD 10,000 in addition to the USD 200.

Payment instructions are not public. Submit reports and request instructions at
the contact email above. A payment is due only after the Licensor has privately
provided payment instructions; until then, royalties accrue but payment is not
late. If instructions arrive after the normal payment deadline, the Licensor
and developer must agree in writing on a payment date for the accrued balance.
Monthly reporting remains due on the normal schedule.

The Licensor may expressly waive a payment or agree to a lower amount in
writing, including by email. Such an agreement must identify the Game and the
amounts or reporting periods covered. Unless expressly stated otherwise, it
does not waive reporting, future royalties or any other developer's obligations.
Silence, a delayed response or failure to request payment is not by itself a
waiver of accrued royalties.

After the threshold is reached, refunds/chargebacks reduce the royalty-bearing
receipts base and can generate a credit against future royalties, not a cash
refund. Do not collect the catch-up royalty a second time if receipts later
drop below and recross the threshold. Total royalties cannot be negative.

This is a contractual royalty, not a tax. You are responsible for reporting
and payment; the Engine does not automatically withhold store payouts.
If law requires withholding from a royalty payment, remit the withholding to
the appropriate authority and provide evidence to the Licensor.

5. Reporting

Before a Game first reaches USD 10,000 in cumulative lifetime Receipts, you
are not required by this license to register the Game with the Licensor,
notify the Licensor of its revenue, submit revenue reports, or disclose sales
figures, store statements, invoices, bank records or other financial evidence.
You remain responsible for tracking the threshold yourself. Once it is reached,
the first report must include the cumulative total, including earlier Receipts;
separate reports for earlier below-threshold months are not required.

Starting with the month the threshold is reached, submit a monthly statement
within 30 days after month end identifying the Game, monthly and cumulative
Receipts, deductions, exchange rates, royalty calculation and credits. Report
zero-receipt months as well. Send reports to the contact email above and use
the payment instructions privately supplied by the Licensor. Keep sufficient
records to substantiate statements. This license grants no separate contractual
audit access or special late-payment penalties. Rights and remedies provided
by applicable law remain unaffected.

For clarity, capitalized "Receipts" means the monetary amounts defined in
section 4, not documentary receipts or proof of sales. A monthly statement
is your own report; this license does not routinely require attaching store
statements, invoices or bank records to it.

The Licensor may agree in writing, including by email, to accept your declared
figures on a trust basis without supporting financial documents. Such an
agreement must identify the Game and the periods covered, or expressly state
that it continues for future periods. It may simplify or waive the reporting
details above, or waive reports entirely, if expressly stated. No supporting
documents need be provided to the Licensor for the agreed scope. Keep records
needed to calculate royalties and satisfy applicable law; this does not require
sending those records to the Licensor under the trust arrangement.

For example, the Licensor may accept your declaration that a Game generated
USD 13,000 in cumulative Receipts, waive documentary proof for that period,
and privately provide payment instructions. Without a separate royalty waiver
or reduction, the total royalty remains USD 260, less royalties already paid
and applicable credits. Accepting self-reported figures does not itself waive
payment, forgive inaccuracies, change the royalty basis or exempt other Games.
Merely supplying payment instructions is not a waiver of reporting; any waiver
must be express. Statutory obligations and legally compelled disclosures are
unaffected.

6. Voluntary contributions

Contributing is optional. You may propose your changes to the official OpenDLE: Studio
repository. You retain copyright in your original contribution.
Before merging a contribution, the Licensor must obtain your explicit agreement
granting a worldwide, perpetual, irrevocable, non-exclusive, royalty-free right
to use, modify, reproduce, distribute, sublicense and relicense that contribution,
including in future commercial releases with different license/payment terms.
An unsolicited patch or pull request alone is not treated as that agreement.
Only grant rights you control, and identify all third-party code and notices.

7. License updates, acceptance and future versions

There is no annual expiry or renewal requirement. The Licensor may publish
updated Engine license terms, including different royalty rates, thresholds
or payment terms, or a different governing law, for future development-tool use.
The conditional update policy below applies even
when you continue using an older Engine version rather than upgrading it.

Activation condition: the notice-and-acceptance mechanism described below is
not currently implemented. Until it is implemented and actually presents
updated terms to you, your accepted permissions continue without a time limit;
publication of new terms alone does not suspend tool use or force acceptance.
There is no current online-check, registration or periodic renewal requirement.

After that mechanism is implemented, when an updated license is presented to
you through its notice
procedure, continued development-tool use outside the educational fallback below
requires your express acceptance of those updated terms. Until that acceptance,
stop tool use outside that fallback; you may still access the license notice
and acceptance interface to review or accept the update. Silence, an online
check, downloading terms or merely displaying them is not acceptance.

Educational fallback: if you decline or have not accepted updated terms, you
may continue using your already-obtained Engine version solely for private,
non-commercial learning, teaching and research under your last accepted
license, subject to this fallback's narrower scope. You may create and run
private exercises and prototypes for those purposes. This permission has no
annual expiry and does not require accepting the updated terms.

The fallback does not permit monetization, paid development services,
development for commercial release, or new public game releases, including
free releases. It does not grant rights to redistribute Engine source or
independent forks, or to obtain later Engine versions. Educational or non-profit
labels alone do not authorize activities outside this scope. Broader tool use
requires express acceptance of the updated terms or a separate agreement.
Existing payment and reporting obligations are not waived by entering this
fallback, and the protected rights for already-released builds below remain.

The update trigger is presentation through that notice procedure, not the
passage of time or merely editing an undisclosed website or repository file.
An unavailable licensing service does not itself constitute notice or
acceptance. Updates do not retroactively change accrued royalties or the
protected game rights below. Mandatory protections under applicable law remain
unaffected by this continued-use condition or the notice procedure.

Game builds publicly released before updated terms are presented to you are
grandfathered: those already-released builds may continue to be distributed,
sold and played under the license terms under which they were released,
including the original royalty rates, thresholds, reporting requirements and
zero-receipts exemption. Future receipts from those builds remain governed by
those original terms. Declining an Engine license update does not disable their runtime or
require players to accept a new Engine license.

Except for private exercises and prototypes within the educational fallback,
developing or publishing new games, updates, ports or DLC using the Engine
after updated terms are presented requires acceptance of the update or a
separate agreement. These new releases follow the updated accepted terms;
they do not retroactively relicense grandfathered builds or their receipts.
Accepting updated terms does not reset a Game's cumulative receipts or charge the initial
threshold royalty twice. Where differently licensed releases of the same Game
coexist, the updated agreement must specify how bundled/shared receipts are allocated
before those releases are commercially distributed; do not silently apply a
new rate to grandfathered receipts.

Planned implementation: the tool will check the current license online and
display changed terms for express acceptance. Online checking, acceptance
records, update notification and offline behavior are not
implemented by this document and must be finalized before this policy is
activated. No automatic acceptance or undisclosed future terms are authorized.
Third-party rights and agreed contribution grants remain applicable.

